\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The Wilson term and odd periods}
\date{2026-11-23}

\p{Use the ordered frame of \ref{fdip-014S} on representatives
with #{N_j\ge3}. On an edge in direction #{j} away from the
seam, the two frames differ by inserting or deleting
#{\varepsilon_j^S}. In the relative product
#{\Upsilon_x^{-1}\Upsilon_{x+e_j}} the common factors cancel,
leaving #{\pm\varepsilon_j^S}. Thus the forward block of the
transformed [Wilson Laplacian](fdip-009U) has this spin
factor tensored with the invertible link #{U_j(x)^{-1}}
and multiplied by #{a^{-2}}. If this block were spin identity times a fiber map,
canceling the link and absorbing the nonzero scalar would give
#{\varepsilon_j^S\otimes\bbI_F=\bbI_S\otimes B} for a fiber
map #{B}. A nonscalar self-adjoint involution has both signs
as eigenvalues. Evaluating on its two eigenspaces would force
#{B=\bbI_F} and #{B=-\bbI_F} on the nonzero fiber. The backward neighbor is distinct and no other hop
contributes to this block. For a nonzero Wilson coefficient
the scalar centered hop on that ordinary edge cannot cancel
the nonscalar spin dependence.}

\p{If #{N_j} is odd, both #{N_j-1} and #{0} are even.
The two frames at the seam therefore coincide. The transformed
centered hop there has spin factor
#{\Upsilon_x^{-1}\varepsilon_j^S\Upsilon_x
=\pm\varepsilon_j^S}. Each generator conjugates this spin factor to
itself or its negative. If this generator is nonscalar, the specified
frame retains a nonscalar seam block. Every generator of the
Pauli realization in \ref{fdip-00LB} is nonscalar. In one
dimension the Hermitian module with #{\varepsilon_1^S=\bbI_S}
instead has scalar spin hops from the outset.}
